#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114904 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4312362-9961-5bf7-9423-42f4c2f7a13c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Area of a Triangle Given an Angle and the Length of a Leg"}] :question/id #uuid "33326641-c2fa-53db-8ff5-8dedde2a16b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-3274" :question/problem "In square units, what is the area of $\\triangle ABC$ shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1608/Source/Images/ebf269e328b3f7a4ca08f735383c0865.png)" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac 1 2 AC \\cdot BC\n$$\n\nNotice that relative to the given angle $\\angle B$, we have the length of the opposite side $\\overline{AC}$ and need to determine the length of the adjacent side $\\overline{BC}$. Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $BC{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (m∠B) &= \\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\\n\\tan (60^{∘}) &= \\frac{9}{BC} \\\\\n\\sqrt{3} &= \\frac{9}{BC} \\\\\n\\sqrt{3}BC &= 9 \\\\\nBC &= \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 9 \\cdot (3\\sqrt{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{27\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114905 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51739685-2d53-5c9b-870b-b557ffdf2768" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle by Solving for a Variable Given a Trigonometric Ratio of One Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "60a0dba7-3007-5958-987a-d262091813cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3277" :question/problem "Given that $\\tan(m\\angle{B}) =\\dfrac{4}{3}$, calculate the area (in square units) of $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1608/Source/Images/546392e4617aab57cfd9f51874ce6f0d.png)" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac12 BC \\cdot AC\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\tan(m\\angle{B}) = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that relative to $\\angle{B}$, we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overline{AC}$ and adjacent side $\\overline{BC}$. Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (m∠B) &= \\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\\n\\frac{4}{3} &= \\frac{2x + 2}{2x} \\\\\n4 \\cdot 2x &= 3 \\cdot (2x + 2) \\\\\n8x &= 6x + 6 \\\\\n2x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= 2x + 2 = 2(3) + 2 = 8, \\\\\nBC &= 2x = 2(3) = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}BC \\cdot AC \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114906 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437a71fe-92e0-58e3-9f49-6560b2755776" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Area of a Triangle Given a Trigonometric Ratio of an Angle and the Length of a Leg"}] :question/id #uuid "514f238c-bac6-516a-a498-d1569db22471" :question/math-academy-id "e-3279" :question/problem "Given that $\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac 13$, calculate the area (in square units) of $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1608/Source/Images/33c414d6627752865e76dbefa5f35222.png)" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac12 AC \\cdot BC\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac13\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\theta = m\\angle{A}\n$$\n\nNotice we're also given the length of the side $\\overline{BC}$ opposite to $\\angle{A}$. Hence, we can use the sine ratio to find the length of the hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= \\frac{BC}{AB} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3} &= \\frac{5}{AB} \\\\\nAB &= 5(3) \\\\\nAB &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the missing side $AC$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= \\sqrt{AB^{2} - BC^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{15^{2} - 5^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{225 - 25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{200} \\\\\n&= 10\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}AC \\cdot BC \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 10\\sqrt{2} \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 25\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114907 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe83dedc-3034-5e3a-b546-dd57c0ccc6e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle by Solving for a Variable Given an Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "759db275-8647-5bf1-a3fb-dc014c920279" :question/math-academy-id "e-3282" :question/problem "In square units rounded to one decimal place, find the area of $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1608/Source/Images/163510a23b9b562d8948b40bb29b7c12.png)" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac12 AC \\cdot BC\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nm\\angle{B} = 23^\\circ\n$$\n\nNotice that relative to $\\angle{B}$, we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overline{AC}$ and adjacent side $\\overline{BC}$. Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (m∠B) &= \\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\\n\\tan (23^{∘}) &= \\frac{x - 1}{2x} \\\\\n2x \\cdot \\tan (23^{∘}) &= x - 1 \\\\\n1 &= x - 2x \\cdot \\tan (23^{∘}) \\\\\n1 &= x(1 - 2 \\cdot \\tan (23^{∘})) \\\\\nx &= (1)/(1 - 2 \\cdot \\tan (23^{∘})) \\\\\nx &= 6.6203…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= 2x = 2(6.6203…) = 13.2406…, \\\\\nAC &= x - 1 = (6.6203…) - 1 = 5.6203…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}(5.6203…)(13.2406…) \\\\\n&= 37.2080… \\\\\n&\\approx 37.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437a71fe-92e0-58e3-9f49-6560b2755776" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Area of a Triangle Given a Trigonometric Ratio of an Angle and the Length of a Leg"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81fc652a-7b2e-5e4a-bb19-34d97e267d54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112965} {:answer/id #uuid "c28e0fb5-4670-5e52-9ae2-450d3a466cd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112966} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ee358c-5b7c-5d72-aa41-732ac755dfa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112967} {:answer/id #uuid "ec89c962-70eb-5412-821e-5ea2810632d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112968} {:answer/id #uuid "3724b346-77c6-50d5-99b4-57984a07eab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec89c962-70eb-5412-821e-5ea2810632d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112968} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cf81eb9-84ef-5c58-aad1-0caf0bab5fc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112964}] :question/id #uuid "fe383b7e-4b92-5b53-b9c3-d588227c5371" :question/math-academy-id "q-28265" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1608/Source/Images/q-28265.png)\n\nGiven that $\\sin θ = \\frac{1}{2}$, calculate the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51739685-2d53-5c9b-870b-b557ffdf2768" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle by Solving for a Variable Given a Trigonometric Ratio of One Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e9ff682-e6a3-5a1e-b09d-964aaf36badc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112971} {:answer/id #uuid "96c49518-4a7a-53ea-8629-db3a4c9be64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112972} {:answer/id #uuid "a32c5bfc-50fc-5a03-9aa3-5df5fa1e368e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112973} {:answer/id #uuid "fd84e58d-4914-5ca0-a80d-d2f85492f631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112974} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce36751-9eb4-5207-99b6-5c7f349581b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e9ff682-e6a3-5a1e-b09d-964aaf36badc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112971} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d35dbef-9fbc-52c1-bcf9-0f525ec88cec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186112970}] :question/id #uuid "f0a77aee-e012-5d50-a91c-7eccfeb5ef38" :question/math-academy-id "q-28266" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1608/Source/Images/q-28266.png)\n\nGiven that $\\tan (m∠A) = 5$, calculate the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above."}]}
